      "writtenOn": "2024-08-13T23:02:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are no traits involved here - this `.as_ref()` calls an \"inherent\" method of `std::result::Result\u003cT, E\u003e`: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/result/enum.Result.html#method.as_ref\n\n`self.0.err()` wouldn\u0027t have worked, because `Result::err` takes `self` by value (and we only have `\u0026self` - an immutable reference).  `self.0.as_ref()` works around that by constructing a **new** result from `\u0026self` (`\u0026Result\u003cT, E\u003e` becomes `Result\u003c\u0026T, \u0026E\u003e`).\n\nPS. If traits were involved then one way to discover trait impls is to look at autogenerated docs - e.g. https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#trait-implementations lists explicit trait `impl`s of a `Vec\u003cT\u003e`:\n\n* There is `impl Write for Vec\u003cu8\u003e` here: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#impl-Write-for-Vec%3Cu8,+A%3E\n\n* And there is also `impl Read for Vec\u003cu8\u003e` although this is a bit more indirect:\n\n    - `Vec\u003cT\u003e` can dereference into `\u0026[T]`: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#impl-Deref-for-Vec%3CT,+A%3E\n    - And there is `impl Read for \u0026[u8]`: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.slice.html#impl-Read-for-%26%5Bu8%5D (reading mutates the slice in a way similar to `span \u003d span.subspan(offset)`)",
      "parentUuid": "e3705bb6_dcd906f9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 112,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 112,
        "endChar": 23
